From: j7 fb
Subject: j7: show the picture behind a near-black desktop

The wallpaper never appeared because the test that decides what is background used

an exact compare against 0xff000000, and neither the lock screen nor the home

screen paints exactly black - they paint a near-black such as 0xff0a0a0a - so

every frame was copied through untouched. Treat any opaque colour whose brightest

channel is nearly nil as background, and shorten the run that qualifies from 48

pixels to 32 for the same reason: the UI is a field of dark panels, not one flat

sheet, so most runs are now a few hundred pixels rather than a full row.

The picture itself is unchanged and still travels compiled into the kernel.

The present now also reports the picture's size and its first and middle pixel and

how many background pixels it replaced, six times. The only evidence until now was

the screen, which cannot distinguish "no picture loaded" from "picture loaded, no

dark pixel ever drawn".

Co-Authored-By: dipa-studio <183933817+dipa-studio@users.noreply.github.com>

diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
--- a/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/arm/SweetFramebuffer.cpp
@@ -894,6 +894,8 @@
 static uint32_t *sweet_fb_wall;
 static int32_t sweet_fb_wall_w, sweet_fb_wall_h;
 static int sweet_fb_wall_tried;
+static uint64_t sweet_fb_wall_px;   /* pixels the last present took from the picture */
+static int sweet_fb_wall_reports;
 
 #if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_A5XELTE)
 /*
@@ -1059,13 +1061,20 @@
 					end++;
 				}
 				v = in[c];
-				if (v == 0xff000000u && (end - x) >= 48) {
+				/* The desktop is not exactly black: the lock screen and the home screen
+				 * paint a near-black (0xff0a0a0a and friends), which the exact test used
+				 * to miss, so no wallpaper ever appeared. Any opaque colour whose
+				 * brightest channel is almost nil is background. */
+				const uint32_t cr = (v >> 16) & 0xffu, cg = (v >> 8) & 0xffu, cb = v & 0xffu;
+				const uint32_t cmax = cr > cg ? (cr > cb ? cr : cb) : (cg > cb ? cg : cb);
+				if ((v >> 24) == 0xffu && cmax <= 0x14u && (end - x) >= 32) {
 					/* A wallpaper area is hundreds of pixels across, a glyph stem is one
-					 * or two, so wide runs of opaque black are background. Paint the
-					 * image there when one is loaded, the gradient when it is not. */
+					 * or two, so wide dark runs are background. Paint the image there
+					 * when one is loaded, the gradient when it is not. */
 					if (sweet_fb_wall != NULL && sweet_fb_wall_w == w &&
 					    sweet_fb_wall_h == h) {
 						v = sweet_fb_wall[(uint64_t)r * (uint64_t)w + (uint64_t)c];
+						sweet_fb_wall_px += (uint32_t)(end - x);
 					} else {
 						v = bg;
 					}
@@ -1158,6 +1167,19 @@
 		sweet_fb_wall_load((int32_t)fLogicalW, (int32_t)fLogicalH);
 	}
 #endif
+#if defined(ARM64_BOARD_CONFIG_A5XELTE)
+	/* Say what the picture is and how much of the panel it replaced: without this the
+	 * only evidence was the screen, and a dark frame could mean either "no picture" or
+	 * "the client never drew a dark pixel". */
+	if (sweet_fb_wall != NULL && sweet_fb_wall_reports < 6) {
+		sweet_fb_wall_reports++;
+		IOLog("A5XELTE FB: wall %dx%d px0=%08x mid=%08x took %llu background pixels last frame\n",
+		    sweet_fb_wall_w, sweet_fb_wall_h, sweet_fb_wall[0],
+		    sweet_fb_wall[(uint64_t)(sweet_fb_wall_h / 2) * (uint64_t)sweet_fb_wall_w +
+		    sweet_fb_wall_w / 2], (unsigned long long)sweet_fb_wall_px);
+		sweet_fb_wall_px = 0;
+	}
+#endif
 
 	static uint32_t sweet_fb_reclog;
 	if (sweet_fb_reclog < 8) {
